// Rep Max questions: Design and Technology (AQA 8552), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in provenance/ted-gcse/questions/.
{"id":"gdt-001","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":1,"q":"What is planned obsolescence?","o":["Designing a product so it can be upgraded for many years","Designing a product so its useful life is deliberately limited","Making a product only from recycled materials","Making products in a factory with no workers"],"a":1,"x":"Planned obsolescence makes people replace products sooner, for example by short battery life or by regular new models. It creates waste and uses more resources."}
{"id":"gdt-002","s":"dt","t":"3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is a CNC router (CAM) useful for making a batch of 200 identical chair parts?","o":["It removes the need for any digital design","It makes each part slightly different for variety","It works without any setting up or checking","It cuts every part to the same accurate size from a digital file"],"a":3,"x":"Computer-aided manufacture follows a CAD file, so every part is cut to the same accurate shape. It is fast, repeatable and accurate for batch production."}
{"id":"gdt-003","s":"dt","t":"3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"How does a pumped storage hydroelectric scheme store energy?","o":["Water is pumped uphill at low demand and sent through turbines at high demand","Stored water is burned to make steam at times of peak demand","Water is pumped uphill at high demand and sent through turbines at low demand","Electricity is stored in the water as chemical energy"],"a":0,"x":"Spare electricity when demand is low pumps water up to a high reservoir, storing gravitational potential energy. At peak demand the water flows back down through turbines to generate electricity."}
{"id":"gdt-004","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a smart material?","o":["MDF","Glass reinforced plastic (GRP)","Thermochromic pigment","Kevlar"],"a":2,"x":"Smart materials react to a change in their environment, such as heat, light or pressure. MDF is a manufactured board, GRP is a composite and Kevlar is a technical textile."}
{"id":"gdt-005","s":"dt","t":"3.1.3","lv":2,"q":"Why is carbon fibre reinforced polymer (CFRP) described as a composite material?","o":["It is a single pure element found in nature","It combines carbon fibres with a resin to give high strength for low weight","It is a metal alloy made of carbon and iron","It is a smart material that changes shape when heated"],"a":1,"x":"A composite is made from two or more different materials joined to give properties neither has alone. CFRP is strong and light, so it is used for bike frames and aircraft parts."}
{"id":"gdt-006","s":"dt","t":"3.1.4","lv":1,"q":"A security light has a PIR sensor, a microcontroller and a lamp. Which part is the output?","o":["The PIR sensor","The microcontroller","The mains power supply","The lamp"],"a":3,"x":"In a systems approach the sensor is the input, the microcontroller is the process and the lamp is the output."}
{"id":"gdt-007","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is an example of a first order lever, with the pivot between the effort and the load?","o":["A pair of scissors","A wheelbarrow","A fishing rod","A pair of tweezers"],"a":0,"x":"In a first order lever the pivot is between effort and load, as in scissors or a seesaw. A wheelbarrow is second order (load in the middle). Fishing rods and tweezers are third order (effort in the middle)."}
{"id":"gdt-008","s":"dt","t":"3.1.5","lv":3,"q":"A 15-tooth driver gear turns clockwise at 120 rpm. It drives a 30-tooth idler gear, which drives a 45-tooth output gear. How does the output gear turn?","o":["40 rpm anticlockwise","60 rpm anticlockwise","40 rpm clockwise","360 rpm clockwise"],"a":2,"x":"An idler does not change the speed ratio, so output speed = 120 × 15 ÷ 45 = 40 rpm. It does reverse direction: driver clockwise, idler anticlockwise, output clockwise."}
{"id":"gdt-009","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a non-ferrous metal?","o":["Low carbon steel","Aluminium","Cast iron","High carbon steel"],"a":1,"x":"Ferrous metals contain iron. Low carbon steel, cast iron and high carbon steel are all iron-based. Aluminium does not contain iron, so it is non-ferrous."}
{"id":"gdt-010","s":"dt","t":"3.1.6.2","lv":1,"q":"Which property describes a material that can be hammered or pressed into shape without cracking?","o":["Ductility","Toughness","Hardness","Malleability"],"a":3,"x":"Malleable materials can be hammered or rolled into shape. Ductile materials can be drawn into wire. Toughness is resistance to impact, and hardness is resistance to scratching or denting."}
{"id":"gdt-011","s":"dt","t":"3.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A designer chooses timber that carries the FSC logo. Which factor mainly influenced this choice?","o":["Environmental and ethical sourcing","Lowest possible cost","Highest possible strength","Quickest delivery time"],"a":0,"x":"FSC certified timber comes from forests managed responsibly, which is an environmental and ethical consideration. Certification does not guarantee the lowest cost or highest strength."}
{"id":"gdt-012","s":"dt","t":"3.2.2","lv":3,"q":"A diving board is fixed at one end and a diver stands at the free end. Which statement describes the forces in the board?","o":["The top surface is in compression and the bottom surface is in tension","Both surfaces are in compression","The top surface is in tension and the bottom surface is in compression","Both surfaces are in torsion"],"a":2,"x":"A fixed one-ended beam bends downwards under the load. The top surface is stretched (tension) and the bottom is squashed (compression)."}
{"id":"gdt-013","s":"dt","t":"3.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which of the six Rs means choosing not to buy a product or material that harms the environment?","o":["Reduce","Refuse","Repair","Recycle"],"a":1,"x":"The six Rs are Rethink, Refuse, Reduce, Reuse, Repair and Recycle. Refuse means saying no to products or materials that cause unnecessary harm."}
{"id":"gdt-014","s":"dt","t":"3.2.4","lv":2,"q":"What does a life cycle assessment (LCA) of a product look at?","o":["Only the cost of making it","Only how it is made in the factory","How popular it is with customers","Its environmental impact from raw materials to disposal"],"a":3,"x":"An LCA looks at every stage: getting raw materials, manufacture, transport, use and disposal or recycling, to measure the total environmental impact."}
{"id":"gdt-015","s":"dt","t":"3.2.5","lv":1,"q":"Why is timber seasoned before it is used to make furniture?","o":["To reduce its moisture content so it is less likely to warp or split","To make it darker in colour","To increase its moisture content so it bends easily","To make it grow faster"],"a":0,"x":"Freshly cut timber contains a lot of water. Seasoning dries it, which stops it shrinking, warping and splitting after the product is made."}
{"id":"gdt-016","s":"dt","t":"3.2.6","lv":3,"q":"A 2400 mm × 1200 mm sheet is cut into 500 mm × 400 mm panels, all the same way round (none turned). Ignore the width of the saw cuts. What is the greatest number of whole panels?","o":["14","15","12","8"],"a":2,"x":"4 panels fit along 2400 (4 × 500) and 3 along 1200 (3 × 400), so 12. The other way round gives 6 × 2 = 12 too. Dividing areas gives 14.4, but whole panels must fit along each edge."}
{"id":"gdt-017","s":"dt","t":"3.2.7","lv":2,"q":"A factory makes 500 chairs of one style, then changes the machine settings to make 500 of a different style. What type of production is this?","o":["One-off production","Batch production","Mass production","Continuous production"],"a":1,"x":"Batch production makes a limited number of identical products, then changes to another product. One-off makes a single product. Mass and continuous production run the same product without changeover."}
{"id":"gdt-018","s":"dt","t":"3.2.8","lv":2,"q":"What is the main purpose of a jig?","o":["To draw the outline of a shape on the material","To measure the surface finish of a part","To heat a material before it is bent","To hold the work and guide the tool so every part is made the same"],"a":3,"x":"A jig holds the work and guides the cutting tool, which gives repeatable accuracy. A template is used to mark out a shape."}
{"id":"gdt-019","s":"dt","t":"3.2.9","lv":1,"q":"Which treatment protects timber used outdoors by forcing a preservative deep into it under pressure?","o":["Tanalising","Seasoning","Galvanising","Annealing"],"a":0,"x":"Tanalising forces preservative into timber under pressure, protecting it from rot and insects. Seasoning dries timber, galvanising coats steel with zinc and annealing softens metal by heating it."}
{"id":"gdt-020","s":"dt","t":"3.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is primary data about what users want from a product?","o":["A magazine article about similar products","Online reviews of a rival product","Results of a questionnaire you give to potential users","A published report of a national survey"],"a":2,"x":"Primary data is collected first-hand by the designer, for example by interviews and questionnaires. Articles, reviews and published surveys are secondary data."}
{"id":"gdt-021","s":"dt","t":"3.3.2","lv":1,"q":"What does the Fairtrade mark on a product show?","o":["The product is made from recycled materials","Producers were paid a fair price and had better working conditions","The product was made in the UK","The product has no carbon footprint"],"a":1,"x":"Fairtrade is about social and economic fairness: fair prices and better working conditions for producers, often in developing countries."}
{"id":"gdt-022","s":"dt","t":"3.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Which company is best known for the bagless vacuum cleaner that uses cyclonic separation?","o":["Alessi","Apple","Anglepoise","Dyson"],"a":3,"x":"James Dyson developed the bagless cyclonic vacuum cleaner. Alessi is known for kitchenware, Apple for consumer electronics and Anglepoise for its adjustable lamp."}
{"id":"gdt-023","s":"dt","t":"3.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A designer keeps returning to her first idea and cannot think of different solutions. What is this called?","o":["Design fixation","Iterative design","User-centred design","Collaboration"],"a":0,"x":"Design fixation is getting stuck on one idea. Iterative design means repeatedly testing and improving. User-centred design focuses on the needs of users."}
{"id":"gdt-024","s":"dt","t":"3.3.5","lv":2,"q":"In a third angle orthographic drawing, where is the plan (top) view drawn in relation to the front view?","o":["Below the front view","To the left of the front view","Above the front view","To the right of the front view"],"a":2,"x":"In third angle projection each view is placed on the same side as the face it shows, so the plan view is above the front view. The right side view is drawn to the right."}
{"id":"gdt-025","s":"dt","t":"3.3.6","lv":1,"q":"What is the main reason for making a prototype?","o":["To sell to customers as the final product","To test the design and find problems before production","To avoid needing a design specification","To increase the number of products made"],"a":1,"x":"A prototype lets designers test function, fit and appearance and make improvements before spending money on production."}
{"id":"gdt-026","s":"dt","t":"3.3.7","lv":2,"q":"Why might a designer choose standard components, such as ready-made hinges and screws, instead of designing new ones?","o":["They can be made to any size needed","They avoid any need for fixings","They are always made from the lightest material","They are cheaper and easy to replace"],"a":3,"x":"Standard components are made in large numbers, so they cost less and are easy to buy and replace. This reduces cost and saves design time."}
{"id":"gdt-027","s":"dt","t":"3.3.8","lv":3,"q":"A part is drawn as 60 mm with a tolerance of +0.3 mm and −0.1 mm. Which range of lengths is acceptable?","o":["59.9 mm to 60.3 mm","59.7 mm to 60.1 mm","59.7 mm to 60.3 mm","59.9 mm to 60.1 mm"],"a":0,"x":"Upper limit = 60 + 0.3 = 60.3 mm. Lower limit = 60 − 0.1 = 59.9 mm. A part within these limits is acceptable."}
{"id":"gdt-028","s":"dt","t":"3.3.9","lv":1,"q":"Why does a designer nest shapes close together when marking out a sheet of material?","o":["To make the shapes stronger","To increase the number of cuts needed","To reduce waste","To leave the sheet looking neater"],"a":2,"x":"Nesting fits shapes together so less material is left over, which saves cost and resources."}
{"id":"gdt-029","s":"dt","t":"3.3.10","lv":1,"q":"Which hand tool is best for cutting tight curves in thin timber?","o":["Tenon saw","Coping saw","Hacksaw","Panel saw"],"a":1,"x":"A coping saw has a thin, narrow blade that can turn corners. A tenon saw and a panel saw cut straight lines, and a hacksaw is for metals."}
{"id":"gdt-030","s":"dt","t":"3.3.11","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a wasting process?","o":["Gluing two pieces of timber together","Steam-bending a strip of timber","Casting pewter in a mould","Drilling a hole in a piece of timber"],"a":3,"x":"Wasting removes material, as in sawing, drilling and planing. Gluing adds material, bending deforms it and casting reforms it."}
{"id":"gdt-031","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a hardwood?","o":["Oak","Pine","Spruce","Larch"],"a":0,"x":"Oak is a hardwood from a broad-leaved tree. Pine, spruce and larch are softwoods from conifers."}
{"id":"gdt-032","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":2,"q":"Which statement about MDF is correct?","o":["It is a natural hardwood","It is very water resistant, so it is ideal outdoors","It has no grain and a smooth surface that is good for painting","It is made by peeling thin veneers and gluing them"],"a":2,"x":"MDF is made from fine wood fibres and resin. It has no grain and a smooth surface, but it swells when it gets wet. Plywood is the board made from veneers."}
{"id":"gdt-033","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":2,"q":"Why are the layers of plywood glued with the grain of each layer at right angles to the next?","o":["So the board is weaker and easier to cut","So the board is strong in both directions and resists warping","So the board can be bent into tight curves","So the board weighs less than solid timber"],"a":1,"x":"Alternating the grain direction gives strength in more than one direction and reduces warping and splitting, unlike solid timber."}
{"id":"gdt-034","s":"dt","t":"mat-tb","lv":3,"q":"A school wants a garden bench that needs little maintenance. Which choice is best justified?","o":["MDF, because it has no grain and is cheap","Balsa, because it is light and easy to cut","Untreated pine, because softwood always lasts outdoors","Teak, because its natural oils resist rot"],"a":3,"x":"Teak is a hardwood with natural oils that resist moisture and rot. MDF swells in water, balsa is soft and weak, and untreated pine can rot outdoors."}
{"id":"gdt-035","s":"dt","t":"nea-A","lv":1,"q":"Which activity belongs to the first part of the NEA, identifying and investigating design possibilities?","o":["Choosing a contextual challenge and researching users' needs","Testing the finished prototype against the specification","Making the final prototype","Producing final working drawings"],"a":0,"x":"The NEA starts with choosing one of AQA's contextual challenges and investigating the needs of users and the opportunities for design."}
{"id":"gdt-036","s":"dt","t":"nea-B","lv":2,"q":"Which is the best specification point for a desk tidy?","o":["It should look attractive to the people who use it","It must be made to a good quality standard","It must hold at least six pens and be no taller than 120 mm","It should be easy and comfortable to use"],"a":2,"x":"A good specification point is measurable or testable, so you can check it at the evaluation stage. The other statements are vague."}
{"id":"gdt-037","s":"dt","t":"nea-C","lv":1,"q":"Which best shows good practice when generating design ideas?","o":["Drawing one idea in great detail","Sketching a range of ideas with notes on each","Copying an existing product exactly","Writing a long description with no drawings"],"a":1,"x":"The stage rewards a range of creative ideas communicated clearly, with notes explaining how each could meet the brief and specification. One idea is not a range."}
{"id":"gdt-038","s":"dt","t":"nea-D","lv":2,"q":"A student makes a card mock-up of a chosen design to check its size and shape. Which NEA stage is this?","o":["Identifying and investigating design possibilities","Writing the design brief","Analysing and evaluating","Developing design ideas"],"a":3,"x":"Modelling and testing a chosen idea, then improving it, is part of developing design ideas, before the final prototype is made."}
{"id":"gdt-039","s":"dt","t":"nea-E","lv":2,"q":"Which is the safest way to prepare to use a pillar drill?","o":["Clamp the work down, tie back long hair and wear eye protection","Hold the work with your hand so you can feel it","Wear thick gloves to protect your hands","Remove the guard so you can see the drill bit clearly"],"a":0,"x":"Work should be clamped. Loose clothing, long hair and gloves can be caught by rotating parts. Guards must stay in place and eye protection should be worn."}
{"id":"gdt-040","s":"dt","t":"nea-F","lv":2,"q":"Which is the best way to evaluate your finished prototype?","o":["List only the things that went well","Change the specification to match the prototype","Test it against each point in your specification","Ask one friend whether they like it"],"a":2,"x":"AQA rewards testing every aspect of the prototype against the brief and specification, backed by feedback from users. The results then show what to improve."}
